  "protocol": {
    "M2a_split": {
      "hypothesis": "Rare decisive signals (a store gap, a late entry, a batch, an enrolment jump) are seldom chosen by random splits among continuous features. Scoring them on their own and taking the larger of the two calibrated scores lets either kind of evidence list a school.\n",
      "change": "An Extended Isolation Forest (one-sided, ndim 2) on the continuous features fed_vs_own, fed_vs_peers, uptake, cereal_ratio, pulse_ratio, usage_trend, round_share, fed_steadiness, after_hours_share; and for count_gap_change (low), entry_delay, entry_batch and enrolment_change (high), each value's tail share among the training window's values. Both are put on one scale as -log10 of the training tail share (the share of training school-days scoring at least as high; ties broken by the larger raw excess). A school-day's score is the larger of the two; nothing is added or weighted. The weekly score, threshold and store-shortfall listing are unchanged.\n"
    },
    "M2b_split_no_shortfall": "M2a with the store-shortfall listing removed (the sparse score carries the count gap)"
  }
